     (user) interface as(VIRTUAL) from KEYS toward TAXONOMY]

His press: *"See if you pick-up the clues about significance of space… Modulo the inline test all
three produce nearly identical virtual type/kind."*

## 1 · SPACE is the binder, and its ABSENCE is what makes quoting optional

  specimen 1   `as VIRTUAL`     spaced  — operator, then a separate operand
  specimen 3   `as(VIRTUAL)`    BOUND   — operator applied to its argument, one group
  specimen 1   `"(virtual) a TERM…"`  spaced INSIDE quotes — literal prose, nothing applied
  specimen 3   `(virtual)taxonomy`    BOUND — a cast, C-style: the qualifier applies to the word

**A `(` that follows a word character with no space is an OPERATOR. A `(` preceded by a space is
LITERAL.** That single rule is what lets specimen 3 drop its quotes: `(visual or user facing)` and
`(user)` are spaced, so the parser walks over them as text; `as(` cannot occur in ordinary English,
so it is an unambiguous terminator even in the middle of unquoted prose.

**Which answers his second question.** The unquoted run "collection or reusable … a (user)
interface" is bounded exactly the way the quoted one is — it OPENS on the bound cast `(virtual)`
and CLOSES on the bound operator `as(VIRTUAL)`. Quotes and binding are two delimiters for one job.
And that is the shop law made good on: **FILE BY WORDS says quoting must never be load-bearing.**
Specimen 3 is the proof that it isn't — the same declaration, with the quotes taken away.

## 2 · CASE says whether to RESOLVE

  UPPER  VIRTUAL · KEYS · TAXONOMY · HOST · TERM · TAG · THEMES     → resolve against the taxonomy
  lower  as · from · toward · into · offering · intern · plural     → QL keywords, never looked up
  lower  type · kind · theme · skin                                 → the name being MINTED, literal

Specimen 1 shows both cases of one word in a single line: `(TAG)` at the head resolves; `tag` inside
`(for C4: tag)` is literal. Same for `(VIRTUAL)` (resolve the mode) against `(virtual)` (a literal
qualifier cast onto the next word).

**And it explains why specimen 1 is the long one.** `TYPE` is minted in UPPERCASE, which collides
head-on with the resolve convention — so it must carry `intern:["type","TYPE"]` and
`plural:["types","TYPES"]` explicitly to say *these spellings are all this one thing.* Mint in
lowercase, as `kind`, `theme` and `skin` do, and the case-fold and the plural come for free. That is
his "continued catering to brevity in the default/most likely cases": **the terse form is not a
shorthand for the verbose one — the verbose one is what you pay for choosing the harder case.**

## 3 · `word` — the undefined vtype that makes the grammar TOTAL

Specimen 1's definition says a TYPE is "a TERM (for C4: tag) **or word**". `word` is invoked and
never defined, and the magic is that it is the **fallback**: any run that does not resolve is a
`word`, and a `word` contributes ITSELF, literally, to the usage string.

That is what lets an unquoted definition be reassembled byte-exact for the `T0ST … TS0T` compare.
Every token either resolves to its usage or stands for itself, so nothing can fail to be
representable — **`to_literal(to_tokens(x)) == x` is total, not merely usual.** Without `word` the
round trip would have an error case; with it, it has none.

**Found by accident, worth recording:** `h/tokenize.sh`, written the same day before this ruling,
already says *"Bare word runs pass through as themselves; everything else is NAMED."* I implemented
`word` without knowing it had a name. The instrument agreed with the grammar before I could.

## 4 · The same shape, one layer up

`bastian` (the panel/crawler) treats an unparseable line as a COMMENT rather than an error — his
words, *"when there are not (valid) instructions (or the parser can't make them out, which is the
same) then we treat as comment."* **That is `word` at the transport layer.** One rule, three
heights: a token that will not resolve is a `word`; a line that will not parse is a comment; a
representation that cannot be checked is reported, never asserted. The grammar has no error state
because it always has somewhere honest to put what it could not read.

## What I have NOT decoded, and am not guessing at

`&1` reads as the first capture. `++TAG<-K:C4` reads as the terse routing tail — register as a TAG,
sourced from K, scoped to C4 — the compressed twin of `as VIRTUAL from KEYS toward TAXONOMY`.
`THEMES(L<-T)` reads per his own note: L's LINK facet fetching T's TAG facet, printed to `1>`.
Whether `++` means append-to-register or something narrower was not pressed. Banked, not claimed.
